# Project-critical build configuration. The live sdkconfig is UNTRACKED —
# every setting this project depends on must live here or it silently reverts
# on a config regen (scripts/check_config_drift.py guards against that).
#
# Hardware truths for the ESP32-S3 module — PSRAM octal mode, 64 KB data
# cache, QIO flash @16 MB, SPIRAM allocator — live in
# sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3. ONE owner per option: this file carries the
# chip-agnostic project policy only. (The per-chip defaults for seven other
# chips were blink-template residue, deleted 2026-09-05.)

# 5. Memory-allocator measurements (the options themselves live in the
#    esp32s3 file; the paid-for measurement notes live here)
#    DO NOT trim CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_STATIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM. Tried 10 -> 6 on
#    hardware 2026-07-27 expecting ~6 KB back (they are DMA-capable internal,
#    the right pool). It went BACKWARDS: largest free block at wifi-up fell
#    from 12,288 to 8,192 B in std mode, gating httpd in a configuration that
#    previously started fine. Fewer static buffers pushes WiFi onto its
#    DYNAMIC_RX_BUFFER_NUM=32 pool, and that churn fragments the heap far worse
#    than a few large blocks allocated once at init. Reverted to the IDF
#    default of 10. If anything, the lever worth testing is reducing the
#    DYNAMIC count, not the static one.
#
#    NOT SET, and deliberately so — both were tried on hardware 2026-07-27 and
#    MEASURED to change the largest free internal block at wifi-up by exactly
#    zero (12,288 B before and after, three builds):
#      CONFIG_SPIRAM_MALLOC_ALWAYSINTERNAL=4096
#      CONFIG_SPIRAM_TRY_ALLOCATE_WIFI_LWIP=y
#    The block that httpd needs is DMA-capable internal RAM (I2S descriptors,
#    WiFi static RX buffers) which cannot be moved to PSRAM, so general-heap
#    knobs cannot reach it. The httpd startup problem is fixed by ORDER instead
#    — httpd_start now runs BEFORE esp_wifi_init() (wifi_manager.c), while the
#    heap is still whole. (An earlier note here named a
#    web_server_reserve_httpd_stack() at the top of app_main: that variant was
#    tried and BOOT-LOOPED — it starved SD/I2S/USB init — and does not exist.)
#    Re-evaluate the two allocator knobs only against a sustained-transfer
#    test, never against boot heap: on that basis TRY_ALLOCATE_WIFI_LWIP is
#    now set (see sdkconfig.defaults.esp32s3).

# 6. Native USB via TinyUSB (CDC console + fast transfer). The console is driven
#    manually over TinyUSB CDC0 (esp_tusb_init_console) — NOT the built-in REPL —
#    so the primary console must be NONE: it must NOT pre-bind stdio fds 0/1/2, or
#    the Feb-2026 fd-rotation bug (stdin=1/stdout=2/stderr=0) returns. esp_tusb is
#    the single thing that binds 0/1/2, after CDC is up.
#    NOTE: sdkconfig is gitignored. After changing these, DELETE the live sdkconfig
#    and rebuild so a stale CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_USB_SERIAL_JTAG cannot resurrect.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_NONE=y
# Pin the secondary console OFF too. By default Kconfig resolves a secondary
# USB-Serial-JTAG console; with TinyUSB owning the PHY it cannot work, and leaving
# it enabled lets a stale CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_*_USB_SERIAL_JTAG resurrect on regen.
CONFIG_ESP_CONSOLE_SECONDARY_NONE=y
CONFIG_TINYUSB_CDC_ENABLED=y
# Single CDC: console + raw-binary fast transfer share CDC0 (a 2-CDC composite
# destabilized CDC0 on the dev host; throughput is the same on one channel).
CONFIG_TINYUSB_CDC_COUNT=1
CONFIG_TINYUSB_CDC_RX_BUFSIZE=512
# 8 KB TX FIFO (was 512 B originally, 16 KB 2026-07-06..08): the flow-controlled
# xfer writer parks a FreeRTOS tick per FIFO-full retry, so 512 B/tick capped the
# link (~512 KB/s at 1 kHz tick; ~51 KB/s on a drifted 100 Hz build — Dan's
# throttled-live repro). 8 KB keeps the ceiling >= ~800 KB/s even at a 100 Hz
# tick (live stream needs 375 KB/s; floor 4 KB asserted in serial_xfer.c).
# Trimmed from 16 KB 2026-07-08: internal RAM exhaustion broke httpd — see
# "Internal RAM budget" in docs/ARCHITECTURE.md before growing this again.
CONFIG_TINYUSB_CDC_TX_BUFSIZE=8192

# 7. Custom partition table (2MB app partitions + audio_store raw partition)
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM=y
CONFIG_PARTITION_TABLE_CUSTOM_FILENAME="partitions.csv"

# 8. 1ms FreeRTOS tick (default 100Hz/10ms). Lets the USB fast-transfer feed loop
#    wait ~1ms (not 10ms) on a full CDC TX FIFO -> ~5x throughput. Audio is I2S-DMA
#    driven, unaffected by tick rate.
CONFIG_FREERTOS_HZ=1000

# 9. FATFS (SD recording path). Mirrored from menuconfig — the live sdkconfig is
#    untracked, so anything project-critical must live here.
#    - 4096-byte max sector (FF_MAX_SS) for the SD streaming write path
#    - two volumes: SD (drive 0) + spiflash (drive 1)
#    - LFN buffers on heap
CONFIG_FATFS_VOLUME_COUNT=2
CONFIG_FATFS_LFN_HEAP=y
CONFIG_FATFS_MAX_LFN=255
CONFIG_FATFS_SECTOR_4096=y

# 9f. LwIP TCP window. WAS 65535 (11.4x the IDF default) "to sustain
#     SD-download throughput" — but that is what BROKE the download: 64 KB of
#     TCP segments per socket allocate internal-first (SPIRAM_MALLOC_
#     ALWAYSINTERNAL=16384), against a steady state of ~18 KB free / 7 KB
#     largest once httpd + WiFi are up, so a sustained transfer starves the
#     heap and stalls. Measured 2026-09-06: download of a 2,304,044 B file
#     stopped at 1,409,024 B. 11680 = 8 x 1460 (8 MSS) — still a deep enough
#     window to keep the pipe full at this link rate, small enough to fit.
#     Judge any change here against a sustained transfer, never boot heap.
CONFIG_LWIP_TCP_SND_BUF_DEFAULT=11680
CONFIG_LWIP_TCP_WND_DEFAULT=11680

# 9b. ISR stack: the pre-regeneration sdkconfig carried 2096 (vs the 1536 IDF
#     default). With SDMMC + I2S + TinyUSB + WiFi ISRs stacked up, keep the
#     larger value rather than risk an intermittent ISR stack overflow.
CONFIG_FREERTOS_ISR_STACKSIZE=2096

# 9d. Event-loop task stack. The IDF default (2304) overflowed during WiFi
#     bring-up: WiFi/IP event handlers ESP_LOG through the TinyUSB CDC console
#     path (log hook + newlib vfprintf + custom VFS), which is heavier than the
#     stock console path. Proven by coredump: "stack overflow in task sys_evt"
#     panic-looped the board ~2 s after association, every boot.
CONFIG_ESP_SYSTEM_EVENT_TASK_STACK_SIZE=4608

# 9c. Crash forensics: with the primary console NONE (TinyUSB owns stdio) panic
#     output goes nowhere, so persist panics to the coredump partition (64 KB
#     tail of flash) and report the reset reason in the console banner.
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_ENABLE_TO_FLASH=y
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_DATA_FORMAT_ELF=y
CONFIG_ESP_COREDUMP_CHECKSUM_CRC32=y

# 9e. Core isolation: core 1 is reserved for the real-time audio pipeline
#     (rx=11 / dsp=9 / tx=12). Pin the remaining floating system tasks to
#     core 0 — most importantly lwIP's tcpip task (priority 18!), which with
#     no affinity can land on core 1 and preempt the whole audio chain during
#     WiFi traffic. (WiFi driver + esp_timer + main are already core 0.)
CONFIG_LWIP_TCPIP_TASK_AFFINITY_CPU0=y
CONFIG_FREERTOS_TIMER_TASK_AFFINITY_CPU0=y

# 10. WiFi brownout mitigation: cap the radio's maximum TX power at the PHY level
#     (IDF default: 20 dBm). Full-power association-retry bursts were collapsing a
#     marginal USB supply (POWERON reset loop). 13 dBm cuts
#     peak TX current substantially and still gives ample same-room range. The
#     runtime default is lower still and tunable: see `wifi power` in web_server.c.
CONFIG_ESP_PHY_MAX_WIFI_TX_POWER=13
# 11. WiFi RX hot path moved from IRAM to flash (2026-10-05). ESP-IDF v5.5.5 grew
#     static internal RAM by 4.9 KB, which halved the largest free internal
#     block at wifi-up (14,336 to 7,680 B on Rev A, AP mode). This frees
#     10.3 KB of IRAM: wifi-up measured 24,119 free / 15,872 largest, above
#     the pre-upgrade figure. It costs some WiFi RX throughput, not yet
#     measured on a clean link. See the 2026-10-05 entry in the internal RAM
#     measurement log (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md).
# CONFIG_ESP_WIFI_RX_IRAM_OPT is not set
# USB descriptor branding (2026-07-06 design review): the board previously
# enumerated as "Espressif Device" from "Espressif Systems" with serial
# "123456". Ship-facing polish: identify as SigPro Labs RadioSonic.
# NOTE: takes effect on the next full build (idf.py fullclean or delete
# build/ after changing defaults; sdkconfig must be regenerated).
# Per-unit unique serial (from chip MAC) is a code change, tracked in the
# design-review report.
# These three strings belong to the product identity listed in
# main/product_identity.h: a fork given to others changes them too
# (TRADEMARKS.md).
CONFIG_TINYUSB_DESC_MANUFACTURER_STRING="SigPro Labs"
CONFIG_TINYUSB_DESC_PRODUCT_STRING="RadioSonic"
CONFIG_TINYUSB_DESC_CDC_STRING="RadioSonic Console"
